on \code{tidymodels.org}.

The \code{fit()} and \code{fit_xy()} functions have arguments called
\code{case_weights} that expect vectors of case weights.
}

\subsection{Controlling penalty values}{

\code{glmnetcr()} computes coefficient estimates over a path of penalty
values. The package can interpolate probabilities between adjacent path
values. Penalties outside the fitted path are clamped to the nearest
endpoint rather than extrapolated.

We suggest setting the penalty path when fitting the model. This is
important when tuning the model because different data sets and mixture
values pair best with different penalty ranges.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{library(ordered)

penalties <- 10^seq(-4, 0, length.out = 20)

ordinal_reg(penalty = tune()) |>
  set_engine("glmnetcr", path_values = !!penalties)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{multi_predict()} can return predictions for the full path. When a
penalty is not supplied to prediction, \code{criteria = "bic"} selects a path
step; \code{criteria = "aic"} is also available in that case.
}

